  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAn amplified upper pattern remains in place this morning. The upper \nlow that was over the Southwest yesterday has weakened into an open \nwave as it now lifts northeast over the Plains. In association with \nthe broader scale wave, water vapor imagery depicts multiple pockets \nof ascent, associated with batches of showers and thunderstorms. One \nof these rounds of weak convection is just exiting the area, while \nanother is beginning to lift northeast from the OK/TX Panhandles. So \nconfidence is increasing that we'll see more scattered showers and \nthunderstorms throughout the day today, beginning mid to late \nmorning. This should keep widespread clouds around, limiting \ninstability. So potential for any strong to severe storms continues \nto decrease, and the prior SPC marginal risk has been trimmed well \nsouth of the area.\n\nSunday into Monday still look quieter as upper ridging sets up over \nthe Plains. However can't completely rule out some drizzle or light \nshowers though, as guidance has come into better agreement in \nkeeping some moist easterly flow in place in the lower levels. Have \nnudged down high temperatures for this period as well, given the \npotential for increased cloud cover.\n\nAttention then turns to the Tuesday through Thursday timeframe. \nGuidance remains consistent in a deep upper low developing over the \nSouthwest and shifting slowly east into the Plains. This low looks \nto interact with and then pick up a recurving Hurricane Polo, \nabsorbing a large plume of tropical moisture. Ensemble mean PWAT is \nstill approaching 2\" by Tuesday night, near record values for this \ntime of year. This is very favorable for heavy rain and potential \nflooding somewhere across the central and southern Plains. However, \nthere is a lot of spread in the exact speed/track of the upper low, \nas well as the embedded remnants of Polo. This will be the key \nfactor in where the heaviest prolonged rain sets up. LREF 25th-75th \npercentiles are roughly 2-3\" through Friday, which seems reasonable \nfor the most likely rainfall amounts at any one point. Spread out \nover several days, these values would be less impactful. Looking at \nthe LREF 90th percentile though, values of 5-7\" correspond well to \ndeterministic guidance as a reasonable worst case for locally higher \npockets. These pockets, with very isolated even higher values, would \nbe more concerning for potential flooding. Pinpointing 4-6 days out \nwhere these higher values will be is an exercise in futility, so for \nnow will continue to watch larger scale trends with the overall \nsynoptic setup. Temperatures throughout this period will be \ndependent on the overall coverage and timing of precipitation, but in\ngeneral the cloudy and moist airmass should keep diurnal temperature\nranges low. Expecting cool daytime highs in the 70s with warm \novernight lows in the 60s.",
